## 3. Mocking Ethics & Anti-Patterns

To keep unit suites resilient across major refactors, teams must adhere to the following mocking rules:

<div class="portal-table-wrap">
  <table class="portal-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Practice</th>
        <th>Status</th>
        <th>Rationale &amp; Enforcement</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Never Mock Data Classes / Structs</strong></td>
        <td>Strictly Prohibited</td>
        <td>Value models (`Order`, `UserSession`, `Money`) must be instantiated as real objects with test fixtures. Mocking data models hides field contract violations.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Mock Boundaries, Not Internals</strong></td>
        <td>Mandatory Standard</td>
        <td>Mock interfaces at architectural boundaries (`OrderRepository`, `AuthService`). Never mock internal helper methods of the class under test.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Deterministic Test Reset</strong></td>
        <td>Mandatory Standard</td>
        <td>Always reset mocks, dispatchers, and state in `@AfterEach` (JUnit), `tearDown` (XCTest), or `beforeEach` (Jest) to eliminate cross-test state leakage.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Zero Real Network Egress</strong></td>
        <td>Strictly Prohibited</td>
        <td>Any test calling live endpoints (`api.enterprise.com`) will be blocked by CI network sandbox rules. Use MockWebServer or URLProtocol stubs.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

